  "language": "en",
  "text": "3.3 Do you live, study, or work in a large city? Are the traffic problems so serious they often cause you to be late? If so, you're not alone. Over the past century, there's been a huge increase in the traffic that moves through our cities. And if, as the United Nations predicts, 70% of the world's population will be living in cities by the year 2050, what will we do when the amount of traffic increases even more? How will we cope? Can we do anything to control it? With me in today's podcast is Mark Knolling, an expert in transport and traffic management. Mark, what's happening in our cities? It's a fact that in a great many cities, the traffic is more extreme than ever before. The primary cause is that there are so many private vehicles on the roads carrying only one or two people. Compare this to buses, which are by far the most efficient option for reducing traffic, as they can transport huge numbers of passengers at the same time. But people don't seem to want to leave their expensive cars at home and take the bus instead. What other factors influence how well traffic flows? Well, a crash on a busy city center road can have a huge impact on all the other roads close by, or someone parking their vehicle so that it blocks the traffic even if only for a minute or so. A delivery driver, for example, can cause trouble for everyone behind them. Even something small like that means everyone's journey suddenly becomes more difficult than they expected. It's the most annoying thing in the world. Should we blame car drivers for all this then? It's not always their fault. Traditional traffic signals in so many of our cities are years old now. They always change color after a certain number of seconds. That's not a sensible idea when we consider the number of vehicles driving today. For instance, a driver might see a green light and continue, but immediately join a long line of vehicles that isn't moving. Then, when the lights turn back to red, they are parked across a busy junction. That usually leads to all sorts of delays over the day.",
